// src/vm.h's FRAMES_MAX (1024) bounds nested Lox calls with a controlled
// runtime error, not a real machine stack fault: src/vm.cpp's own
// interpret() pushes the top-level script as CallFrame 0 before the body
// runs, so 1022 nested calls from within the script is the deepest that
// stays under the ceiling, and 1023 overflows it (verified against the
// release-preset native binary, build/loxpp). A backend whose calling
// convention recurses its own host call stack has no such ceiling on its
// own, and must build one to match, or this depth silently succeeds where
// native fails.
//
// Lives directly in translation-probes/, not in a per-target subdirectory:
// both the JVM backend (runtime/jvm/src/lox/LoxClosure.java) and the CLR
// backend (runtime/clr/src/LoxClosure.cs) mirror this ceiling, so
// tools/diff_runtimes.py's CI probes step, which walks this directory
// comparing native against each managed backend, sees this program agree
// with native (both fail with empty stdout) on both.
fun down(n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    return down(n - 1);
}
print down(1023);
